= 2 The tide

The tide of the Ashwind Sea is semidiurnal: two high waters and two low waters
in a tidal day of 24 h 50 min. Every high water therefore arrives about fifty
minutes later than the one on the day before.

The tidal range is the height of high water minus the height of low water. At
Torvig Harbour the mean range is 3.4 m, the spring range 5.1 m and the neap
range 1.7 m — the spring range is exactly three times the neap range.

Between low water and high water the water rises for six hours, and the rise is
not even. The tide office teaches the rule of twelfths: one, two, three, three,
two and one twelfth of the range in the six successive hours. Half the range is
reached after three hours.

Spring tides occur two days after new moon and two days after full moon, twice
in a synodic month of 29.5 days. That two-day lag is the age of the tide.

