= 3 The flag signal code

#figure(
  image("../raw/images/tide-chart.png", width: 80%),
  caption: [The rule of twelfths, as posted in the harbour office],
)

Before the radio link, the harbours talked to each other with flags from the
signal masts. Six flags are in use:

#list(
  [Grey — mail boat sighted],
  [Blue — pilot requested],
  [Red — harbour closed, do not enter],
  [White — doctor needed on the island],
  [Black — death in the settlement],
  [Yellow — fresh water short],
)

A flag is valid only while it is hoisted; lowering it is the cancellation. Two
flags on one mast are read from the top down, and the upper flag is the more
urgent message. White over black is forbidden. A flag at half mast means
"understood, help is on the way".

#pagebreak()

= 4 Approaching the harbours

Ovray Cove dries out below 1.2 m above chart datum. Low water at springs is
0.3 m, at neaps 1.6 m, so the cove is unreachable at low water springs and
usable throughout the neaps. Boats bound for Ovray Cove plan their arrival
within two hours either side of high water.

All heights on the charts are given above chart datum, the lowest astronomical
tide. The soundings are a worst case: the real depth is almost always greater
than the chart says.
